DEFINITION MODULE GDT;

(* GDT -- Global Descriptor Table and Task State Segment setup for x86_64.

   On entry from Limine the CPU is already in long mode with a bootloader-
   provided GDT.  This module replaces it with a kernel-owned GDT that
   contains:

       [0] Null descriptor                selector 0x00
       [1] Kernel code  (Ring 0, 64-bit)  selector 0x08
       [2] Kernel data  (Ring 0)          selector 0x10
       [3] User data    (Ring 3)          selector 0x18
       [4] User code    (Ring 3, 64-bit)  selector 0x20
       [5] TSS descriptor low             selector 0x28
       [6] TSS descriptor high            (continuation of [5])

   and a 64-bit Task State Segment (TSS) that provides:
       rsp0     -- ring-0 stack pointer loaded on privilege change to ring 0
       rsp1/2   -- ring-1 and ring-2 stack pointers (unused, set to rsp0)
       ist1..ist7 -- Interrupt Stack Table slots; each points to a dedicated
                     8 KiB stack so critical exceptions (DF, NMI, MC) can
                     switch to a known-good stack automatically.

   The GDT table itself, the TSS, the 32 KiB ring-0 stack, and the seven
   IST stacks are all placed in BSS by this module (gm2 emits them as
   static variables; ADR() and MemZero() ensure the symbols are not
   optimised away).  GDTInit builds the descriptors in place, zeroes the
   GDT/TSS via MemZero, issues LGDT, reloads CS (through a far return)
   and DS/ES/SS, then loads the task register with LTR.

   Call GDTInit() exactly once, early in kernel startup, before enabling
   interrupts or handling exceptions.  Written in pure Modula-2; the few
   privileged instructions (lgdt, ltr, far return to reload CS) are issued
   via inline assembly. *)

PROCEDURE GDTInit();

END GDT.
